{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "bandup",
  /*
    Not `.open-next/worker.js` directly. That file is regenerated by every
    build and its default export has only a `fetch` method, so the Worker had
    no way to run anything on a timer. cloudflare/worker-entry.mjs imports it,
    passes `fetch` straight through and adds the `scheduled` handler for the
    cron below. See that file; it is mostly comment.
  */
  "main": "cloudflare/worker-entry.mjs",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-03-25",
  // nodejs_compat is required by the Next.js runtime on Workers.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  /*
    Something drains the replica queue whether or not anybody is using the app.

    Until this line every drain hung off a learner's own write — two rows at a
    time, and in two of the three call sites filtered to that learner's own
    user id, so a pending row belonging to somebody who had stopped using
    BandUp was not retried slowly, it was never retried at all. Between 14 and
    16 August nothing came and drained the queue, D1 fell two days behind
    Supabase across twelve progress snapshots, twenty usage events, two cost
    events, a profile and a username, and every counter the app had said "4
    pending", which is what a queue about to be drained also looks like.

    Five minutes, and 25 outbox rows plus 50 cleanup keys per run. The
    arithmetic behind those three numbers is in
    lib/cloudflare/scheduled-replica-drain.ts.

    Cron triggers belong to the deployed Worker, not to an uploaded version, so
    this does not fire for a pull-request preview.
  */
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["*/5 * * * *"]
  },
  "limits": {
    // Paid Workers allow this explicit 30-second ceiling. It replaces the
    // Free plan's 10 ms ceiling that terminated ordinary Next.js renders.
    "cpu_ms": 30000
  },
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "BANDUP_DB",
      "database_name": "bandup-data-production",
      "database_id": "50c3ec03-7ee6-446d-b628-ae04c880cdc0",
      "migrations_dir": "cloudflare/migrations"
    }
  ],
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "BANDUP_FILES",
      "bucket_name": "bandup-files-production"
    }
  ],
  "send_email": [
    {
      "name": "EMAIL",
      "allowed_sender_addresses": ["accounts@bandup.life"]
    }
  ],
  "ai": {
    "binding": "AI"
  },
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "AUDIO_GENERATION_RATE_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "99041001",
      "simple": { "limit": 2, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "MODEL_FETCH_RATE_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "99041002",
      "simple": { "limit": 12, "period": 60 }
    }
  ],

  /*
    Do not delete the Worker's variables on the way past.

    Wrangler's default is to treat this file as the only source of truth for
    environment configuration — "like a terraform file", as its own schema puts
    it — so every deploy first deletes every plain-text variable on the Worker
    and then sets whatever `vars` says here. Historically there was no `vars`
    block, so the second half set nothing and the first half deleted
    everything. The tiny block below now contains only an explicit production
    safety kill-switch; dashboard variables remain authoritative for the rest.

    That is not a theoretical footgun. It has now taken production down twice:
    once eating ADMIN_USERNAME, and once eating ADMIN_USERNAME again together
    with ACCOUNTS_ENABLED and all six Stripe price ids — which locked the owner
    out of their own admin account and closed subscriptions on a live site,
    silently, on a deploy that reported success. Encrypted Secret values were
    untouched both times, which is what made it look random.

    DEPLOY.md used to answer this by telling whoever set a variable to mark it
    Secret even when it held nothing secret. That worked and was the wrong fix:
    it asked a person to remember a rule at the exact moment the dashboard is
    offering them the sensible-looking default, forever, and the cost of one
    lapse is a broken production site with no error anywhere.

    So the trap goes instead. With this set, the dashboard is authoritative for
    variables and a deploy leaves them alone. Nothing about which values are
    secret changes — a key still belongs in a Secret, because Secrets are
    encrypted at rest and hidden from the dashboard afterwards. What changes is
    that getting that wrong is no longer a way to delete your configuration.

    tests/deploy-config.test.mjs fails if this is removed.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    One thing the paragraph above promises and did not deliver

    "The dashboard is authoritative for variables" turned out not to be
    dependable. STRIPE_WALLET_METHODS was set in the dashboard, deployed from
    the dashboard, and never reached the Worker: /api/billing/config kept
    answering `["alipay"]`. A deploy's own configuration diff then printed

        vars: {
      -    STRIPE_WALLET_METHODS: "alipay,wechat_pay"
        }
        Uploading the Worker will override the remote configuration with your
        local one.

    which says a dashboard variable absent from this file is on its way out,
    `keep_vars` or not. Whether wrangler deleted it or OpenNext never exposed
    it, the same lesson holds either way: a variable that has to survive a
    deploy belongs in this file, where it is reviewed, previewed and permanent.

    So non-secret configuration goes here from now on. The dashboard is for
    Secrets, which are encrypted and were never affected by any of this.
  */
  "keep_vars": true,
  "vars": {
    "ORGANIZATION_UI_PREVIEW": "0",
    /*
      Which wallets the Alipay/WeChat Pay button offers, and lists on the
      Stripe Checkout Session.

      Only what the Stripe account is actually approved for may appear here.
      Stripe refuses an entire Session that names a method it has not
      activated, so an aspirational entry does not add that method — it takes
      the working one down with it, which is exactly how #122 broke Alipay.
    */
    "STRIPE_WALLET_METHODS": "alipay,wechat_pay",
    // Flipped to "cloudflare" — the one-way write-authority step — only once
    // all nine of the ten cutover-domain registry entries that can be proven
    // statically were (lib/cloudflare/cutover-domains.ts), and only as the
    // owner's explicit, named request, per the working agreement in
    // CLAUDE.md. The tenth, `cutover_write_barrier`, cannot be a static
    // flag — it is live D1 state, and this deploy is only valid alongside
    // arming it.
    //
    // THIS PR MUST NOT MERGE ON ITS OWN. Every Supabase learner writer
    // (lib/auth/supabase.ts) checks cutoverWriteBarrierArmed("learner") and
    // refuses immediately once armed, regardless of what this value says —
    // but the app only reroutes those writes to D1 once this value already
    // reads "cloudflare". Arming the barrier before this deploys, or
    // deploying this long before arming the barrier, both mean a real
    // window where every learner write fails. Arm the barrier via
    // POST /api/admin/cloudflare/write-barrier { domain: "learner", confirm:
    // true }, then merge and deploy this immediately after — not before, not
    // with delay after.
    //
    // Prior state: "read_cloudflare" since 2026-08-23 (see the pull requests
    // that flipped it, reverted it after AVATAR_URL_SIGNING_KEY turned out
    // to be unset, and re-flipped it once that was fixed).
    "CLOUDFLARE_DATA_MODE": "cloudflare",
    "ORGANIZATION_DATA_MODE": "cloudflare",
    // Native Cloudflare identity is active after its D1 mappings and password
    // evidence were verified. Existing browser sessions migrate silently.
    "CLOUDFLARE_NATIVE_AUTH": "1",
    // Explicitly separate from learner-data authority. The native writer is
    // armed only after production subscription, event-payload and prepaid
    // ledger parity has passed; it writes verified Stripe events directly to
    // D1 while the completed learner barrier seals the legacy writer.
    "CLOUDFLARE_NATIVE_STRIPE_BILLING": "1",
    "GOOGLE_OAUTH_APP_ORIGIN": "https://bandup.life"
  }
}
